= \\frac{1}{3}x\n     \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\( y = \\frac{2}{3}x \\) and \\( z = \\frac{1}{3}x \\) into the total weight equation:**\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{1}{3}x = 100\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the terms on the left-hand side:**\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{1}{3}x = 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{3}{3}x = 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x + x = 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2x = 100\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{100}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 50 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Find \\( y \\) and \\( z \\) using \\( y = \\frac{2}{3}x \\) and \\( z = \\frac{1}{3}x \\):**\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 50 = \\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 50 = \\frac{50}{3} \\approx 16.67 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe amount of broccoli needed is approximately 50 grams, the amount of carrots needed is approximately 33.33 grams, and the amount of bell peppers needed is approximately 16.67 grams. Therefore, the vegetable stir-fry requires approximately 50 grams of broccoli, 33.33 grams of carrots, and 16.67 grams of bell peppers.\n\n### Verification:\n- Total weight of the vegetables: \\( 50 + 33.33 + 16.67 \\approx 100 \\) grams\n- Ratio of broccoli to carrots to bell peppers: \\( \\frac{50}{33.33} \\approx \\frac{3}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{50}{16.67} \\approx 3 \\)\n\nThis example demonstrates how the distributive property can be used to solve a problem involving a mixture of different types of vegetables by setting up and solving a system of equations. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed example. This really helps in understanding how to use the distributive property in solving mixture problems involving different types of vegetables.\n\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on applying the distributive property in solving mixture problems or any oth